"""Plain-language reference copy for the released Lens Debaser controls."""

def update(descriptions):
    copy = {
'capture': ('Adjust existing lens effects together using camera and lens settings.', 'Capture Influence controls how much the other settings in this group matter. At zero, the group makes no change. Focal Length and Gate / Capture Format describe the field of view: shorter focal lengths or larger formats strengthen compatible edge distortion, color fringing, softness and falloff. Aperture is an f-number; smaller numbers strengthen compatible bokeh, coma, optical vignette and flare. Shorter Focus Distance strengthens compatible defocus, spherical halo and focus-related color fringing. These settings modify effects already enabled elsewhere. They do not change perspective, recover camera metadata or bring an out-of-focus source into focus.'),
'look': ('Add a coordinated lens look, then fine-tune its individual effects.', 'Look Amount controls all contributions from this group; zero turns them off. Character adds a modest mix of edge softness, fringing, darkening and highlight glow. Vintage Bias makes the image warmer, softer and less contrasty; Vintage Caricature exaggerates that character. Exotic Bias adds more irregular geometry, color separation and uneven edge behavior. Classic 2x Anamorphic Bias adds oval bokeh, cylindrical distortion and horizontal flare. You can combine these controls. They add to the individual effect settings, so a macro can produce an effect even when its individual amount slider is at zero. Anamorphic character does not desqueeze footage.</p><p>You can use a Look Macro as a starting point, or apply it after adjusting the individual controls to push your existing treatment toward a different lens character. The order in which you adjust these settings does not change the result. Macros leave your individual settings unchanged: their contributions are combined with your settings when the image is rendered, pushing the response toward the selected lens character. Increasing or reducing a macro’s bias changes how strongly that character influences the image. Be aware that Look Macros can also add to or subtract from parameter values you have not adjusted yourself. Your settings remain intact: for example, the UI can still show an untouched amount slider at zero even though a macro adds a nonzero amount behind the scenes. Set Look Amount to zero to remove all macro contributions and see the image produced by your individual settings alone.'),
'geometry': ('Bend straight lines and stretch detail around Optical Center.', 'Primary Distortion creates the broad barrel or pincushion bend. Secondary Distortion changes that bend more strongly toward the edges. Moustache Distortion adds a bend that changes direction between the center and edge, giving straight lines a wave. Positive and negative values bend in opposite directions; zero removes that control’s contribution. Geometry Field Mix decides how much these three distortions follow Shared Field: zero affects the whole image, while increasing it protects more of the central area selected there. It does not change Projection. Peripheral Stretch expands or compresses outer detail; Peripheral Warp bends it unevenly. Moving Optical Center moves the center of these distortions. These controls move and resample existing pixels; they cannot reveal detail outside the source image.'),
'field-shape': ('Choose the central area to protect and how effects grow toward the edges.', 'Field Center moves the area around which compatible effects are arranged. Its X and Y values run from 0 at the left or top to 1 at the right or bottom; 0.5 / 0.5 is the center. Field Aspect makes this area wider or taller, and Field Rotation turns it. Field Onset sets where the edge response begins; Field Transition Width controls how gradually it reaches full strength. These settings influence active field focus, directional detail, fringing, pupil and some prism effects, plus distortion when Geometry Field Mix is raised. They do not enable those effects or set Projection Field Angle. Geometric Swirl twists image detail around Optical Center; negative values reverse the twist. It can visibly warp the image on its own and is separate from Bokeh Swirl, which changes defocused highlight shapes.'),
'focus-field': ('Soften or smear the outer image while preserving a clearer central area.', 'Edge Focus Loss blurs outer detail. Field Curvature makes focus vary across the frame as though the lens’s best-focus surface were curved. Astigmatism makes blur differ along directions pointing toward the center and around it. Radial Smear spreads detail toward or away from the center; Tangential Smear spreads it along the circular direction around the center. Shared Field controls where these changes begin and how gradually they grow. Capture Context and Look Macros can change their strength. These are simulated lens blur and smear, not tools for recovering focus or moving a real subject into focus.'),
'detail': ('Make existing texture crisper or softer without changing the composition.', 'Microcontrast changes the separation between light and dark in medium-sized texture. Fine Detail affects smaller texture. Positive values make these details more pronounced; negative values soften them. Sagittal Detail does the same for detail along directions pointing toward the center, while Tangential Detail works along directions around it. Those directional changes grow toward the edges according to Shared Field. Edge Detail Loss removes detail toward the edges. Detail Scale chooses the size of texture affected. The controls use detail already in the source; they cannot restore missing information. Sharpening is reduced in areas already blurred by the focus effects, so it does not simply sharpen the blur back into focus.'),
'chromatic': ('Separate colors at edges or add colored fringes around blurred focus transitions.', 'Red Fringing and Blue Fringing move those color channels relative to the others, most visibly on high-contrast edges away from the center. Negative values reverse the displacement. Chromatic Onset at −1 uses Shared Field; values of 0 or more choose a separate starting region, with Chromatic Transition Width setting how gradually the fringing grows. Anamorphic Fringing adds horizontal color separation. Longitudinal Amount instead adds Near-Focus Color and Far-Focus Color around focus transitions; Longitudinal Radius sets the spread. Its radius and color controls are unavailable when Longitudinal Amount is zero. A connected depth map lets Depth & Focus determine the near/far relationship; without one, the effect uses local image detail. This is colored optical fringing, not a whole-image color grade. Capture Context, Look Macros and active Lens Variation can change the response.'),
'off-axis': ('Give bright points a comet-like tail, strongest away from the center.', 'Coma controls the tail’s strength and length. Positive and negative values point the tail in opposite directions; zero adds no tail. Coma Threshold chooses which bright areas produce it: lower the threshold if the image’s lights are not bright enough. Small lights or reflections against a dark background show the effect best; evenly lit footage may show little change. Shared Field can change the direction of the outer response, while Capture Context and Look Macros can change its strength. Scatter Threshold Softness makes the brightness cutoff more gradual. Coma adds highlight tails rather than shifting the whole image or blurring every edge.'),
'aperture': ('Create shaped defocus and change the appearance of blurred highlights.', 'Aperture Amount enables this group and blends shaped blur with the image. At zero, the dependent controls are unavailable, including Optical Drift. Aperture Radius sets the blur size. This can add blur to sharp footage; it cannot recover focus. With an external depth map, Focus Depth protects the selected distance and defocus grows away from it. Without a map, the lens uses its internal image and field response. Small lights against a darker background make the shapes easiest to judge.</p><p>Aperture Shape chooses Circular, Polygonal or Oval. Blade Count and Blade Curvature apply only to Polygonal: more blades and more curvature make its outline rounder. Aperture Rotation turns Polygonal or Oval shapes and is unavailable for Circular. Pupil Aspect stretches the shape; Edge Softness softens its boundary. Cat-Eye squeezes highlights toward the frame edges. Bokeh Swirl stretches and turns outer bokeh around Field Center, without twisting sharp image detail. Pupil Shift moves the outer highlight shape off-center, Pupil Clipping cuts away part of it, and Pupil Rim Weight makes its outer ring brighter.</p><p>Drift Amount moves the blurred part progressively without moving the sharp source. Negative amounts reverse the direction. Drift Direction selects Radial (outward or inward), Tangential (around the center) or Directed (a chosen angle). Drift Angle is available only for nonzero drift in Directed mode. Shared Field sets the spatial response; Field Focus & Smear, Capture Context and Look Macros can further change the result. Effect Size controls how pixel-sized blur scales when resolution changes.'),
'vignette': ('Darken the edges in three different ways.', 'Natural Vignette adds smooth brightness falloff toward the edges. Optical Vignette simulates shading from the lens opening, with strength influenced by Capture Context. Mechanical Vignette darkens outside the coverage boundary defined by Image Circle. You can combine all three. Optical Center moves the center of the darkening. These controls darken existing pixels; they do not crop the frame. Use Final Framing if you want to crop away unwanted dark edges.'),
'image-circle': ('Set the coverage boundary for Mechanical Vignette.', 'Image Circle Size makes the covered area larger or smaller. Image Circle Aspect makes it wider or taller; Image Circle Softness changes the transition between covered and darkened areas. Optical Center positions the boundary. These controls make no visible change when Mechanical Vignette is zero. They do not change projection, crop the image or supply missing source pixels.'),
'bloom': ('Add a soft glow around bright areas.', 'Bloom Amount sets glow strength; at zero there is no bloom. Bloom Threshold chooses which areas are bright enough to glow. Lower it to include more of the image, or raise it to restrict bloom to the brightest lights and reflections. Bloom Radius sets how far the glow spreads. Bloom Stretch makes it wider horizontally. Scatter Threshold Softness makes the brightness cutoff less abrupt. Effect Size changes how the spread scales with resolution. A dark or low-contrast image may show little bloom until the threshold is lowered. Bloom is independent of Glare and Anamorphic Flare; their thresholds and amounts are separate.'),
'glare-halo': ('Add a broad wash of light or a smaller glow around bright detail.', 'Glare Amount sets the strength of the broad wash. Glare Threshold selects the bright areas that feed it, and Glare Radius sets how far it spreads. Glare Color Amount mixes Glare Color into that added light; zero leaves the added glare untinted. These color controls do not tint the whole source image. With Glare Amount at zero, its radius and color settings have no visible effect.</p><p>Spherical Halo adds a more concentrated glow around bright detail; increasing it also increases its spread. It can work without Glare or Bloom. Spherical Halo selects bright detail automatically rather than using Glare Threshold. Both responses are easiest to see around lamps, reflections or bright windows against darker surroundings. Scatter Threshold Softness controls how gradually highlights enter the response, and a depth map can limit spill across depth boundaries. Capture Context and Look Macros can alter the halo strength. Effect Size scales pixel-sized spread with output resolution.'),
'anamorphic-flare': ('Create horizontal streaks, reflected highlights and vertical rays from bright lights.', 'Flare Amount enables this group and sets its overall strength. At zero, the dependent controls are unavailable. Flare Threshold selects which lights and reflections are bright enough to produce flare; lower it when expected lights produce no response. Flare Color colors the primary flare, while Reflection Color colors the reflections separately. Scatter Threshold Softness controls how gradually lights cross the brightness threshold. Capture Context and Look Macros can change the strength; a depth map can help limit spread between depth layers. Effect Size scales pixel-sized distances with output resolution. This group adds light responses; it does not stretch the whole image or desqueeze footage. Use Anamorphic Geometry for cylindrical distortion.'),
'highlight-response': ('Make the brightness cutoff for glow and flare more gradual.', 'Threshold Softness affects the thresholds used by Bloom, Glare, Spherical Halo, Coma and Anamorphic Flare. Low values keep the cutoff tighter; higher values let nearby brightness levels contribute more gradually. It changes which bright areas feed those effects, not the softness of their final blur. It does not compress highlights or change the whole image’s contrast. Enable one of the affected effects first; this control does nothing visible on its own. Use Transmission → Highlight Softness to soften the image’s highlight tones instead.'),
'transmission': ('Change the image’s color, brightness, contrast and highlight roll-off.', 'Color Amount mixes Transmission Color into the whole processed image; unlike Flare Color or Glare Color, this can tint the entire frame. At zero, the selected color has no effect. Density is measured in stops: positive values darken the image and negative values brighten it. Transmission Contrast increases contrast with positive values and reduces it with negative values. Highlight Softness compresses bright tones while preserving middle gray. It changes brightness, not spatial blur. These controls work without Bloom or Glare, but changing the image’s brightness or color can also change which highlights feed those effects.'),
'variation': ('Give the active lens treatment repeatable, uneven imperfections.', 'Variation Amount controls this group; at zero its other controls are unavailable. Field Asymmetry makes active edge effects less symmetrical. Pupil Irregularity makes an active aperture shape less regular, and requires shaped bokeh. Chromatic Asymmetry makes active color fringing uneven. Transmission Unevenness adds broad differences in light transmission. Variation Seed selects another fixed pattern: the same seed reproduces the same pattern and does not animate it. This group varies the lens treatment; it does not apply a complete look by itself. Enable the relevant field, bokeh or chromatic response to judge its associated variation.'),
'front-wear': ('Add haze, wiping marks, scratches and patches of worn coating.', 'Cleaning Haze spreads light and lowers contrast. Cleaning Marks add curved wiping traces; Deep Scratches add stronger, sparser marks. Scratch Direction sets their dominant angle, and Mark Scale sets their size. Coating Wear creates patches with altered color, contrast and light scatter; Wear Patch Scale sets the patch size. Damage Seed chooses a different fixed pattern. These are lens-surface imperfections, not scratches on film. Bright windows, lamps and reflections reveal the scattered light most clearly; a dim image can make it subtle. The wear controls can produce their own scatter without enabling Bloom or Glare. A depth map can help limit that scatter across depth boundaries.'),
'internal-contamination': ('Add soft cloudy patches and scattered light from deposits inside the lens.', 'Internal Dirt Amount sets the overall visibility; zero disables the contamination. Internal Dirt Size changes the size of the cloudy patches, and Internal Smear elongates them. Internal Scatter adds local glow and softened contrast around bright areas. Cloud Softness smooths patch boundaries; Cloud Complexity adds smaller overlapping patches. Internal Dirt Seed selects another fixed arrangement. Values up to 2 offer a more restrained range; higher amounts exaggerate the deposits. Bright lights show the scatter most clearly, while the density patches can remain visible elsewhere. A depth map can limit scattered light crossing depth boundaries, but it does not move the fixed dirt pattern. This is not film grain or animated noise.'),
'depth': ('Use a grayscale depth map to choose what stays in focus and limit light spill.', 'Connect a depth map to Second RGB/Depth Map and choose the matching Depth Interpretation. Depth-Free ignores that input and uses the internal response. The other modes use the map; without a valid map they cannot provide the intended distance-based control. Input Near and Input Far select the map values that represent the near and far ends. Focus Depth selects the distance to keep sharp, with 0 representing the near end of the interpreted map and 1 the far end. Defocus Onset sets how far from that distance defocus begins; Full Defocus Distance sets where it reaches full strength. The latter is the ending distance, not the width of the transition. Depth Edge Protection reduces blur and scattered light crossing between different depth layers. Depth Edge Softness adjusts tolerance around soft or noisy boundaries. These controls require compatible effects such as Aperture Amount, longitudinal color fringing or optical scatter; a map alone does not add an effect. They do not change geometry or recover sharp detail missing from the source. The workflow below explains the interpretation modes and supported effects.'),
'blend': ('Mix the lens-treated image with the original.', 'Effect Blend at 0 shows the original; at 1 it shows the full lens treatment. Intermediate values mix the two. This changes the overall contribution, not individual blur sizes or thresholds. Final Framing comes after this mix, so Auto Fill or Manual Crop can still crop the image even when Effect Blend is zero.'),
}
    copy.update({
'presets': ('Choose a starting look, load a preset file or save your own settings.', 'Preset applies a look from the menu. Load opens a .ldbpreset file and adds presets from its folder to the menu. An asterisk beside the loaded name means you changed its settings; restoring those settings removes the asterisk. Custom means the current settings are not based on a loaded preset. Save writes the current lens settings to a new .ldbpreset file. Presets leave Input Color Space unchanged, so check that setting separately for your footage.'),
'processing': ('Match the incoming image’s color space and inspect the effect.', 'Input Color Space tells the effect how to read the image entering this node. Choose that image’s actual space, which may differ from the timeline or export space. The default matches DaVinci Wide Gamut / Intermediate. This control does not enable an effect, and presets leave it unchanged. Choosing the wrong space can change brightness, color and which highlights produce glow or flare. The output retains the input encoding.</p><p>Effect Size defaults to Frame Relative: pixel-sized effects such as blur and scatter stay proportionally similar when output resolution changes. Fixed Pixels keeps their size in pixels, so they occupy less of a higher-resolution image. It does not change geometry or overall effect strength, and presets can save the choice.</p><p>Diagnostic View replaces the finished image with a view of a particular response, such as depth or the difference from the source. It is for checking settings, not a finishing effect. Return it to Off for the normal image; the individual views are explained below.'),
'anamorphic': ('Add the uneven geometry associated with an anamorphic lens.', 'Anamorphic Field Aspect changes the proportions of the lens field, influencing how compatible edge effects are arranged. Cylindrical Distortion bends image geometry differently across the horizontal and vertical directions; negative values reverse the bend. These controls do not desqueeze recorded footage. Color separation is controlled by Anamorphic Fringing under Chromatic Aberration, while streaks and reflected lights are under Light & Color → Anamorphic Flare.'),
'projection': ('Change wide-angle perspective and choose how the image fits the frame.', 'Model chooses Equidistant or Stereographic, two different ways of mapping a wide field of view onto a flat image. Off bypasses projection and disables its other controls. Amount (%) blends the new mapping with the existing image. Field Angle is the angle from Optical Center to a frame corner, half the diagonal field of view. Higher angles produce more extreme reshaping; near 89° the image can enlarge heavily. Zero Amount or Field Angle adds no projection. Try around 55° and adjust Amount first.</p><p>Framing chooses how the result fits: Fill Frame enlarges it to keep the frame covered; Balanced trades enlargement against edge coverage; Preserve Centre Scale retains the central size but can expose stretched source edges. Projection resamples existing pixels; it cannot supply detail outside the original frame. Its Field Angle is independent of Shared Field. Final Framing can crop remaining unwanted edges after all the lens effects.'),
'opticsSection': ('Move the center used by projection, distortion, edge darkening, fringing and flare.', 'Optical Center has separate X and Y values: 0 is the left or top edge, 1 is the right or bottom edge, and 0.5 / 0.5 is the image center. Moving it changes where these lens effects are centered; it does not simply slide the whole image. Enable a relevant effect to see the change. Field Center in Shared Field is separate: it positions the area protected from compatible edge effects. Keep both centered for a symmetrical result, or separate them for an uneven lens character.'),
'refractive': ('Add local waves, stretching and color separation as though the glass were uneven.', 'Irregularity Amount sets how strongly detail bends and changes size; zero removes this response. Irregularity Scale sets the size of the warped patches. Edge Bias shifts more of the effect toward the perimeter. Directionality elongates the pattern along an axis, and Direction turns that axis. Irregular Dispersion separates colors along the local bend; it needs nonzero Irregularity Amount. Irregularity Seed chooses another fixed arrangement. It does not animate the pattern or add new image detail.'),
'prism': ('Bend detail and separate colors across a chosen part of the frame.', 'Prism Amount controls the bend; zero disables it and makes its dependent controls unavailable. Prism Dispersion separates colors along that bend. Prism Direction sets its axis, or turns the local direction in Radial Field mode.</p><p>Distribution chooses where it appears: Linear Edge enters from one side; Uniform affects the whole frame; Bilateral / Axis gives matching responses on either side of an axis; Radial Field grows toward the outside; Inverse Field concentrates the response toward the center. Prism Edge Bias positions the one-sided response, and Transition Width softens its entry. Those two controls are available only for Linear Edge. Bilateral, Radial and Inverse use Shared Field to position and shape the response. This moves and resamples source pixels; it cannot reveal content outside the image.'),
'final-framing': ('Crop into the finished image to hide unwanted stretched or dark edges.', 'Auto Fill Frame estimates the crop needed for edges exposed or stretched by lens effects. It is off by default. With it on, Adjust Auto starts at 0%: negative values reduce the estimated crop, positive values increase it. With it off, Manual Crop starts at 0% and lets you choose the crop yourself. Only the relevant slider is available; both settings are retained when switching modes.</p><p>This crops after Effect Blend, so it can still crop at zero blend. It changes composition and enlarges the remaining pixels, without changing the lens effect itself. It cannot repair the unwanted edges or recover missing detail. Extreme distortion, large blur or borders already in the source can need manual adjustment. Use a higher-resolution source when enlarging. Auto Fill adds rendering work, so leave it off if it is unnecessary.'),
    })
    for slug in ('geometry', 'field-shape', 'projection', 'anamorphic', 'refractive', 'prism'):
        lead, body = copy.get(slug, descriptions[slug])
        # Keep the source-detail advice tied to controls that enlarge or resample imagery.
        if 'source footage at a higher resolution' not in body:
            body += ' When these controls enlarge image detail, use source footage at a higher resolution than the output if possible. Otherwise, areas that should stay sharp can soften. Changing timeline resolution alone does not restore missing source detail.'
        copy[slug] = (lead, body)
    descriptions.update(copy)
